zipstream — provenance note

A record only. This file carries NO condition beyond zig-libs' own MIT license,
and `zipstream` is deliberately absent from root NOTICE §1 (the exhaustive list
of condition-bearing files) for that reason.

WHY IT EXISTS AT ALL. The module's implementation is clean-room from the public
PKWARE ZIP spec (APPNOTE.TXT) and the ECMA-376 OOXML package layout, and owes
nobody anything. Its TEST DATA is where the honest answer is longer than one
line, and a provenance statement about source does not answer for data.

  src/testdata/write_golden.zip
      Produced by this repo's own writer from content this repo authored
      ("quick brown fox", "stored payload"). `unzip`/`zipinfo` were run against
      it purely as black-box oracles — the case root NOTICE §0 exempts. Nothing
      third-party is inside the file.

  src/testdata/xlsx_sample.xlsx
      Produced by LibreOffice 26.2.4.2 (`--headless --convert-to xlsx`) from a
      three-line CSV of our own. The CELL CONTENT is therefore ours, but the
      package is not only cell content: LibreOffice also emits its stock
      template parts, and those dominate the file.

          xl/styles.xml       4451 B   LibreOffice's default style table
          xl/theme/theme1.xml 2257 B   LibreOffice's default theme
                                       (`<a:clrScheme name="LibreOffice">`)
          xl/sharedStrings.xml 326 B   our strings
          docProps/app.xml               names the generator explicitly:
                                       `LibreOffice/26.2.4.2$Linux_X86_64`

      So the fixture is NOT "our data that merely passed through a foreign
      tool", which is what this module's README claimed until 2026-08-12. It
      embeds boilerplate XML that LibreOffice authored. Recording that is the
      point of this file.

WHY THIS IS A RECORD AND NOT A CONDITION. LibreOffice is MPL-2.0, but MPL-2.0
governs LibreOffice's own source, not documents LibreOffice generates: The
Document Foundation asserts no copyright over user documents its software
produces, and a stock style/theme table is in any case functional boilerplate
rather than expressive authorship. No redistribution condition attaches to this
fixture, and none is reproduced here. What was wrong was the CLAIM, not the
file — the README said "no third-party source involved" about a module that
ships a third party's generated XML, and a reader checking that sentence
against the artifact would have found it false.

WHY THE FIXTURE IS NOT SIMPLY REGENERATED WITHOUT IT. It cannot be. Every
OOXML writer emits `styles.xml`, and the reader under test exists precisely to
handle real-world packages rather than minimal synthetic ones — a fixture
stripped of the parts a real tool emits would stop testing the thing it is for.

HOW THIS WAS FOUND. Not by reading the fixture's own documentation, which
described it correctly as "produced by LibreOffice from our CSV" and was taken
at face value in a first pass. It was found by opening the ZIP and reading the
XML inside. The lesson is the general one: a file's account of itself is a
claim, and a claim about provenance is checked against the artifact or not at
all.
